    "q-3611": {
      "dom_id": "step-q3611",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the integral of $\\frac{1-3x}{x(x+2)^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-3x}{x(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+2}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x+2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n1-3x=A(x+2)^{2}+B(x)(x+2)+C(x).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-3(0) & =A(0+2)^{2}+B(0)(0+2)+C(0) \\\\ 1 & =4A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-3(-2) & =A(-2+2)^{2}+B(-2)(-2+2)+C(-2) \\\\ 7 & =-2C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-3x & =A(x+2)^{2}+B(x)(x+2)+C(x) \\\\ 1-3(1) & =A(1+2)^{2}+B(1)(1+2)+C(1) \\\\ -2 & =9A+3B+C \\\\ -2 & =9(\\frac{1}{4})+3B+(-\\frac{7}{2}) \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-3x}{x(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4x}-\\frac{1}{4(x+2)}-\\frac{7}{2(x+2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-3x}{x(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{4x}-\\frac{1}{4(x+2)}-\\frac{7}{2(x+2)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{7}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{7}{2(x+2)}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant.",
