    "q-86098": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86098",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}+13x+8}{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+13x+8}{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+4x}=\\frac{5{x}^{2}+13x+8}{x(x+2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+13x+8}{x(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+2}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x+2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+13x+8=A(x+2)^{2}+Bx(x+2)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-2)^{2}+13(-2)+8 & =A(-2+2)^{2}+B(-2)(-2+2)+C(-2) \\\\ 2 & =-2C \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(0)^{2}+13(0)+8 & =A(0+2)^{2}+B(0)(0+2)+C(0) \\\\ 8 & =4A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-1)^{2}+13(-1)+8 & =A(-1+2)^{2}+B(-1)((-1)+2)+C(-1) \\\\ 5-13+8 & =A-B-C \\\\ 0 & =2-B-(-1) \\\\ 0 & =-B+3 \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+13x+8}{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+4x}=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{3}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}+13x+8}{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{3}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{1}{x+2}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant.",
